---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const pattern = [
  0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 2, 1, 0, 1,
  2, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0,
];
---

<Site>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <span class="live-dot"></span> ELECTRONICS · COMPUTING · COMMUNICATIONS
      </p>
      <h1>
        Small lights.
        <br />
        <em>Big ideas.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Learn electronics, computing and networking by making things with
        microcontrollers — small computers you can connect to lights, sensors
        and other devices.
      </p>
      <p>
        Connect a strip of lights. Make a colour move from one end to the other.
        Find out how the circuit, the program and the signals make it happen.
        Each thing you understand gives you another idea for what to build.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button dark" href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
          Try the browser lessons <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/`}>
          Build your first lights →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="light-study">
      <div class="study-label">
        <span>A PATTERN MADE FROM INSTRUCTIONS</span>
        <span>COMPUTER → SIGNAL → LIGHT</span>
      </div>
      <div
        class="led-grid"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A repeating light pattern on a six by six LED grid"
      >
        {pattern.map((n, i) => (
          <span class={`led level-${n}`} style={`--i:${i}`}></span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Which lights should change next?
        <br />A program turns your rule into a pattern.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <aside class="wrap" aria-labelledby="early-testing">
    <div class="testing-note">
      <p class="eyebrow">A WORKSHOP IN PROGRESS</p>
      <h2 id="early-testing">Early testing. A few rough edges included.</h2>
      <p>
        KajLights is an experimental project. Some things work, some are
        unfinished, and some will need another go. The browser lessons are ready
        to try; check the firmware catalogue before buying a board. Hardware
        setup may still need developer tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        Something confusing or broken?{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/KajLights.github.io/issues">
          Tell us what you tried
        </a>
        . Your feedback helps improve the guides and the software.
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>
  <section class="wrap section split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">FROM A WORKING LIGHT TO AN IDEA OF YOUR OWN</p>
      <h2>What could you make with this?</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        A light by your door could mean “come in” or “busy”. A row of lights
        could count down to the start of a game. A moving beam could become a
        lighthouse in a model harbour. These projects start with the same parts,
        used in different ways.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightVM is the software behind the lights. Its editor lets you write
        programs with Python, JavaScript or visual blocks, try them on screen
        and run them on a board. The learning activities help you explore what
        happens from the instructions you write to the light you see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start with an example and change something. Follow the build guide if
        you want to work with real lights, or try the browser lessons with no
        hardware or account. You can explore, investigate and make at your own
        pace.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/`}>
          Find an activity →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">THREE WAYS TO UNDERSTAND YOUR PROJECT</p>
      <h2>
        Connect it. Program it.
        <br />
        Talk to it.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="subjects">
      <article>
        <span class="number">01</span>
        <h3>Electronics</h3>
        <p>
          Connect the power, ground and data wires. Change the colour without
          moving a wire. Discover how electrical power and information do
          different jobs in the same circuit.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/#electronics`}>
          Follow the circuit →
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="number">02</span>
        <h3>Computing</h3>
        <p>
          Make a dot move along a strip. Pause the program and inspect its
          position. Find out how a computer uses instructions, memory and time
          to decide what happens next.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/#computing`}>
          Explore the instructions →
        </a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="number">03</span>
        <h3>Networking & communications</h3>
        <p>
          Save a name on your board over USB, then read it over Wi-Fi. Follow
          the messages between two computers and learn how an address helps them
          reach the right place.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/#communications`}>
          Follow a message →
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section split" aria-labelledby="imagine-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">IMAGINE IT. DESCRIBE IT. TRY IT.</p>
      <h2 id="imagine-title">
        The more you understand, the more you can imagine.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        Once you know a board can keep time and control each light, you can
        imagine a countdown. Once you know it can receive messages, you can
        imagine starting that countdown from another device.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI can help build those ideas. Understanding the parts helps you say
        what you want, ask useful questions and check the result. KajLights aims
        to give you that understanding through things you can try yourself.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${base}about/#why-understanding`}>
        Read about the project’s aims →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section split" id="what-is-a-board">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">MEET THE SMALL COMPUTER</p>
      <h2>What’s a board, anyway?</h2>
      <p>
        The boards used here have a chip called a microcontroller, a USB socket
        and pins for connecting other parts. The microcontroller contains a
        processor and memory. It runs your program and sends instructions to the
        LED strip, which shows the result.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Buy the board. Make the project.</h3>
      <p>
        You don’t need to design a circuit board from scratch. Start with an
        off-the-shelf Raspberry Pi Pico W or Pico 2 W, or one of the supported
        Seeed XIAO ESP32 models. You can buy these from electronics suppliers;
        our hardware guide links to the manufacturers and their retailers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The build guide uses a Pico 2 W and an LED strip. It shows the parts,
        three wire connections and how to run your first colour. An adult helper
        prepares the board and checks the wiring with you.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}hardware/#find-a-board`}>
          Find a board →
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/#parts`}>
          See the starter parts list →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="setup-band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">BUILD YOUR FIRST LIGHTS</p>
        <h2>
          Three wires.
          <br />
          Your first colour.
        </h2>
        <p>
          A Pico, a USB cable and a strip of lights are the starting point.
          Connect them, choose a colour and make a signal someone else can
          recognise. The guide explains each part as you use it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="journey">
        <li>
          <span>1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Gather the bits</h3>
            <p>
              Use the parts list and pictures to find the board, lights and
              wires. Ask your helper to prepare the board.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Connect and set up</h3>
            <p>
              Follow the diagram for power, ground and data. Check the three
              wires together, then plug in USB.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Make something glow</h3>
            <p>
              Choose a colour. Change it. Invent a light signal, or make your
              first moving pattern.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap actions">
      <a class="button dark" href={`${base}build/`}>
        Parts and build guide →
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>
        Already running KajLights? Open your devices →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Site>
